  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# General Metric Spaces\n\nSo far, our topology has lived in $\\mathbb R^n$: open balls come from the Euclidean (or equivalent) norm, and compactness is \"closed and bounded\" (Heine-Borel). But many natural spaces in analysis — function spaces, sequence spaces, spaces of curves — don't sit inside any $\\mathbb R^n$. They're **metric spaces** in their own right, and the theory generalises beautifully.\n\nThis notebook abstracts away from $\\mathbb R^n$ and develops:\n\n1. **The definition** of a metric space with non-Euclidean examples.\n2. **Open sets, closed sets, convergence** — restated for general metrics.\n3. **Completeness** — Cauchy sequences, the completion of a space.\n4. **Compactness** — the equivalence of open-cover, sequential, and \"complete + totally bounded\" in metric spaces.\n5. **Continuous maps** between metric spaces — and the key observation that continuity depends only on which sets are open.\n\nThis is the bridge to functional analysis (Stage 5) and to general topology ([topology-journey](https://github.com/cmsd2/topology-journey)).",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 17,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## 1. The definition\n\nA **metric** on a set $X$ is a function $d: X \\times X \\to [0, \\infty)$ satisfying:\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| (M1) Positivity | $d(x, y) = 0 \\iff x = y$ |\n| (M2) Symmetry | $d(x, y) = d(y, x)$ |\n| (M3) Triangle inequality | $d(x, z) \\le d(x, y) + d(y, z)$ |\n\nThe pair $(X, d)$ is a **metric space**.\n\n### Examples beyond $\\mathbb R^n$\n\n| Space | Metric | Notes |\n|-------|--------|-------|\n| $C([a, b])$ | $d(f, g) = \\sup_{x \\in [a,b]} |f(x) - g(x)|$ | Sup-metric. Convergence = uniform convergence. |\n| $\\ell^2$ | $d(a, b) = \\sqrt{\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty |a_n - b_n|^2}$ | Infinite-dimensional Hilbert space. |\n| Any set $X$ | $d(x, y) = \\begin{cases} 0 & x = y \\\\ 1 & x \\ne y \\end{cases}$ | **Discrete metric.** Every subset is open. |\n| Bounded functions $B(S)$ | $d(f, g) = \\sup_{s \\in S} |f(s) - g(s)|$ | Generalises $C([a,b])$ without continuity. |\n| $\\mathbb R$ with $d(x,y) = |e^x - e^y|$ | (Verify the axioms!) | Same set, different metric, different topology? No — homeomorphic to standard $\\mathbb R$. |\n\nThe discrete metric shows that *any* set can be a metric space. The question is whether the resulting topology is useful.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Demonstrate: convergence in the sup-metric on C([0,1]) */\n/* f_n(x) = x^n converges pointwise to 0 on [0,1) but NOT uniformly on [0,1] */\n/* because sup|f_n - 0| = f_n(1) = 1 for all n (or approaching 1 near x=1) */\n\nxs : np_to_list(np_linspace(0, 1, 200))$\n\ntraces : []$\nfor n in [1, 2, 5, 10, 50] do (\n  ys : map(lambda([xx], float(xx^n)), xs),\n  traces : append(traces, [\n    color=if n < 5 then \"lightblue\" else if n < 20 then \"blue\" else \"darkblue\",\n    name=concat(\"x^\", n),\n    lines(xs, ys)\n  ])\n)$\n\napply(ax_draw2d, append(traces, [\n  title=\"f_n(x) = x^n on [0,1]: pointwise → 0, but sup-norm → 1\",\n  xlabel=\"x\", ylabel=\"f_n(x)\",\n  grid=true, showlegend=true\n]))$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 18,
      "outputs": [
        {
