    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## 2. Topology from a metric\n\nGiven a metric $d$ on $X$:\n\n- **Open ball:** $B(x, r) = \\{y \\in X : d(x, y) < r\\}$.\n- **Open set:** $U \\subseteq X$ is open if $\\forall x \\in U, \\exists r > 0: B(x, r) \\subseteq U$.\n- **Closed set:** complement of an open set.\n- **Closure:** $\\overline{A} = \\{x : d(x, A) = 0\\} = \\{x : \\text{every ball around } x \\text{ meets } A\\}$.\n- **Convergence:** $x_n \\to x$ iff $d(x_n, x) \\to 0$.\n\nEverything from the $\\mathbb R^n$ notebook (1.01) generalises word-for-word. The proofs are identical — they never used anything specific about $\\mathbb R^n$ except the three metric axioms.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## 3. Completeness\n\nA sequence $(x_n)$ is **Cauchy** if $\\forall \\varepsilon > 0, \\exists N: m, n \\ge N \\Rightarrow d(x_m, x_n) < \\varepsilon$.\n\nA metric space is **complete** if every Cauchy sequence converges (to a limit in the space).\n\n| Space | Complete? | Why |\n|-------|-----------|-----|\n| $\\mathbb R$ with standard metric | Yes | Completeness axiom |\n| $\\mathbb Q$ with standard metric | No | $\\sqrt 2$ is a Cauchy limit not in $\\mathbb Q$ |\n| $C([a,b])$ with sup-metric | Yes | Uniform limit of continuous functions is continuous |\n| $\\ell^p$ for $1 \\le p \\le \\infty$ | Yes | Riesz-Fischer |\n| $(0, 1)$ with standard metric | No | $1/n \\to 0 \\notin (0,1)$ |\n\n### The completion\n\nEvery metric space $(X, d)$ embeds isometrically into a complete metric space $(\\hat X, \\hat d)$ — its **completion**. Concretely: $\\hat X$ is the set of equivalence classes of Cauchy sequences in $X$ (two sequences are equivalent if their \"distance\" tends to 0).\n\n- The completion of $\\mathbb Q$ is $\\mathbb R$.\n- The completion of $C_c(\\mathbb R)$ (compactly supported continuous functions) under the $L^p$ norm is $L^p(\\mathbb R)$ — for $1 \\le p < \\infty$. It fails at $p = \\infty$: the sup-norm closure of $C_c(\\mathbb R)$ is $C_0(\\mathbb R)$, the continuous functions vanishing at infinity, which is far smaller than $L^\\infty(\\mathbb R)$.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Demonstrate: a Cauchy sequence in Q that doesn't converge in Q */\n/* Successive rational approximations to sqrt(2) via Newton's method: */\n/* x_{n+1} = (x_n + 2/x_n) / 2 */\n\n/* Start from the integer 1 and stay in exact arithmetic, so every iterate\n   really is a rational — floats would quietly leave Q and hide the point. */\nxr : 1$\napproxs : [xr]$\nfor i : 1 thru 5 do (\n  xr : (xr + 2/xr) / 2,\n  approxs : append(approxs, [xr])\n)$\n\nprint(\"Newton approximations to √2 — every term an exact rational:\")$\nfor i : 1 thru length(approxs) do\n  print(\"  x_\", i-1, \"=\", approxs[i],\n        \"   |x_n - √2| ≈\", abs(float(approxs[i]) - sqrt(2.0)))$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"Cauchy in Q, but the limit √2 is irrational — so Q is not complete.\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 19,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "Newton approximations to √2 — every term an exact rational:\n  x_ 0 = 1    |x_n - √2| ≈ 0.41421356237309515\n  x_ 1 = 3/2    |x_n - √2| ≈ 0.08578643762690485\n  x_ 2 = 17/12    |x_n - √2| ≈ 0.002453104293571595\n  x_ 3 = 577/408    |x_n - √2| ≈ 2.1239014147411694e-6\n  x_ 4 = 665857/470832    |x_n - √2| ≈ 1.5947243525715749e-12\n  x_ 5 = 886731088897/627013566048    |x_n - √2| ≈ 0.0\nCauchy in Q, but the limit √2 is irrational — so Q is not complete."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## 4. Compactness in metric spaces\n\nIn $\\mathbb R^n$: compact = closed + bounded (Heine-Borel). In general metric spaces, \"closed + bounded\" is *not* enough — the closed unit ball in $\\ell^2$ is closed and bounded but not compact!\n\nThe correct equivalence in metric spaces:\n\n**Theorem.** For a metric space $X$, the following are equivalent:\n1. **Compact** (open-cover definition): every open cover has a finite subcover.\n2. **Sequentially compact**: every sequence has a convergent subsequence.\n3. **Complete and totally bounded**.\n\nwhere **totally bounded** means: for every $\\varepsilon > 0$, $X$ can be covered by finitely many $\\varepsilon$-balls.\n\n### Why \"closed + bounded\" fails in infinite dimensions\n\nConsider the sequence $e_1, e_2, e_3, \\ldots$ of standard basis vectors in $\\ell^2$. This is bounded ($\\|e_n\\| = 1$) and lives in a closed set (the unit sphere), but $\\|e_m - e_n\\| = \\sqrt 2$ for $m \\ne n$ — so it has no convergent subsequence.\n\nThe issue: the unit ball in $\\ell^2$ is bounded but **not totally bounded** — you need infinitely many $\\varepsilon$-balls (for $\\varepsilon < \\sqrt 2/2$) to cover it.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Demonstrate: in R^n, we can cover the unit ball with finitely many ε-balls */\n/* In ℓ², we can't. Show the covering number grows with dimension. */\n\n/* How many ε-balls (radius 0.5) does it take to cover [-1,1]^n?\n   Chop each axis into ⌈2/ε⌉ pieces and cover each small cube: that gives an\n   UPPER bound of (⌈2/ε⌉ + 1)^n.  Finite for every n — so [-1,1]^n is totally\n   bounded — but growing exponentially in the dimension. */\neps : 0.5$\nfor n in [1, 2, 3, 5, 10, 20, 100] do\n  print(\"  dim =\", n, \": covering number ≤\", (ceiling(2/eps) + 1)^n)$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"In infinite dimensions, no finite cover exists — not totally bounded.\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 20,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "  dim = 1 : covering number ≤ 5\n  dim = 2 : covering number ≤ 25\n  dim = 3 : covering number ≤ 125\n  dim = 5 : covering number ≤ 3125\n  dim = 10 : covering number ≤ 9765625\n  dim = 20 : covering number ≤ 95367431640625\n  dim = 100 : covering number ≤\n       7888609052210118054117285652827862296732064351090230047702789306640625\nIn infinite dimensions, no finite cover exists — not totally bounded."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## 5. Continuous maps between metric spaces\n\nA function $f: (X, d_X) \\to (Y, d_Y)$ is **continuous at $x_0$** if:\n\n$$\\forall \\varepsilon > 0, \\exists \\delta > 0: d_X(x, x_0) < \\delta \\Rightarrow d_Y(f(x), f(x_0)) < \\varepsilon.$$\n\nEquivalent formulations (same proofs as $\\mathbb R^n$):\n- Sequential: $x_n \\to x_0 \\Rightarrow f(x_n) \\to f(x_0)$.\n- **Topological: the preimage of every open set is open.**\n\nThe third characterisation is the crucial one: **it mentions only open sets, not the metric**. Two different metrics on the same set that produce the same open sets give the same continuous functions. This is why general topology (which axiomatises open sets directly) is the right framework.\n\n### Lipschitz and uniform continuity\n\n| Condition | Definition | Relation |\n|-----------|-----------|----------|\n| Continuous at $x_0$ | $\\delta$ depends on $\\varepsilon$ and $x_0$ | weakest |\n| Uniformly continuous | $\\delta$ depends on $\\varepsilon$ only (not on $x_0$) | stronger |\n| Lipschitz | $d_Y(f(x), f(y)) \\le L \\cdot d_X(x, y)$ for fixed $L$ | strongest |\n\nCompact domain forces uniform continuity (Heine-Cantor theorem — works in general metric spaces, not just $\\mathbb R^n$).",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## 6. What this unlocks\n\nThis notebook provides the foundation for:\n\n- **Functional analysis (Stage 5):** Banach and Hilbert spaces are *complete* normed (hence metric) spaces. The concepts here (completeness, compactness, continuous linear maps) are the starting point.\n- **General topology ([topology-journey](https://github.com/cmsd2/topology-journey)):** The observation that continuity depends only on open sets motivates abstracting away the metric entirely — keeping only the axioms that open sets satisfy.\n- **The Baire category theorem** and **Arzelà-Ascoli theorem** — both need general metric space compactness (not just Heine-Borel).",
      "metadata": {}
    },
